    q: "How does a buy-sell provision in a JV work, and why does it push the partners toward a fair price?",
    a: "One partner names a price for the whole property. The other must either buy the first partner's interest or sell its own, at what each would get if the property sold at that price and the cash ran through the waterfall. The price-setter doesn't know which side it will take, so it has every reason to name a fair price.",
    why: "It's the cake-cutting rule: one cuts, the other chooses. Pricing the property rather than the interests lets the waterfall set each side's share, promote included, as in a real sale. It's usually available only after a lockout or a deadlock, and it favors the partner with ready cash: an operator that can't raise money within the closing period may be forced to sell cheaply. That's why GPs negotiate longer closing periods, and why the buyer usually posts a deposit it forfeits if it fails to close.",
    example: "LP [[90%]], GP [[10%]], a [[$50M]] loan, and capital plus pref of [[$48M]] with a [[20%]] promote above it. The LP names [[$110M]]. Ignoring selling costs, $60M runs through the waterfall: the GP gets $4.8M + $2.4M of promote + $0.96M = $8.16M, and the LP $51.84M. The GP must buy the LP out for $51.84M or sell to it for $8.16M.",
