    why: "Depreciation cut taxable income at ordinary rates, which for high earners exceed 25%, so recapture takes back part of that benefit at a capped rate. The investor usually still comes out ahead: it deducted at a higher rate years earlier and repays at 25% later. Cost segregation changes the math: parts reclassified as personal property are recaptured as ordinary income on a sale. A 1031 exchange defers both the recapture and the capital gain.",
    example: "Bought for [[$20M]], [[$4M]] of depreciation taken, sold for [[$25M]]: adjusted basis $16M, gain $9M. Recapture: $4M × 25% = $1.0M. Capital gain: $5M × 20% = $1.0M. That's $2.0M of federal tax at the top rates, plus $342K of investment income tax at 3.8% of $9M.",
    trap: "Recapture applies to depreciation 'allowed or allowable', so skipping the deduction doesn't avoid it. And a sale below the purchase price can still be taxed: sell for $19M and the $3M gain is all recapture."
