    type: "qa",
    q: "A deal is so far below its pref that the GP's promote looks worthless. How does that change the GP's incentives, and how do LPs respond?",
    a: "The promote is like a call option; far out of the money, the GP has little to lose from risk and something to gain from a long shot. It may hold too long, gamble on a big leasing bet or lose interest. LPs respond with forced-sale and removal rights, or a reset: a new promote on a lower hurdle.",
    why: "Below the hurdle, the GP bears only its co-invest share of any loss, while a lucky outcome could revive the promote, so its incentives diverge from the LP's. Resetting the promote accepts that the old one is gone and pays the GP for recovering value from here, often in exchange for fresh GP capital or lower fees. That's usually cheaper for the LP than replacing an operator mid-plan, which costs time and adds execution risk.",
    example: "[[$40M]] of equity needs [[$50M]] to clear the pref, but the equity is worth about [[$32M]]. The old promote pays nothing unless value rises 56%. A reset might pay [[10%]] of gains above $32M after a [[6%]] return, so the GP earns again from a realistic recovery.",
    trap: "Don't assume a GP below the hurdle wants to sell. Selling makes the loss final and ends its fees, so it often prefers to hold. That's why LPs negotiate forced-sale rights after a set date."
