    a: "For control, cost and fit. In a commingled fund the manager picks the deals and the institution is one LP among many. A separate account (one investor, one manager) or a JV lets the institution approve each deal, tailor the strategy and negotiate lower fees. The price: it takes a big check and in-house staff to pick and oversee deals.",
    why: "A commingled fund offers diversification and access to a manager's full platform with little work, which suits smaller investors and new strategies. Larger investors often want more say: they may have views on markets or property types, want to avoid overlap with their other holdings or need specific terms. Separate accounts and JVs provide that, and the bigger check earns better economics. But control brings responsibility: if the investor approves a bad deal, the fault is partly its own.",
